-- Adds an editable "Scene description" field to Movie (Stage 5), same
-- pattern as Character/Property/Background's one-line description —
-- pre-populated once at Movie-creation time from the scene's own
-- location/action/dialogue/emotion (see index.html's Stage 4 lock
-- handler), then freely editable, never auto-resynced. Without this,
-- the compiled Movie prompt only ever described camera movement
-- (direction/duration/broll/transition), never what's actually supposed
-- to happen or be said in the clip — risking an arbitrary, unusable
-- generation that wastes AI credits.
UPDATE stage_prompts
SET config = json_set(
  config,
  '$.items.movie.content.fieldsSchema', json('[{"key": "scene_description", "label": "Scene description (from Stage 1 — edit if needed)", "type": "textarea"}, {"key": "direction", "label": "Animation direction", "type": "textarea", "presets": ["Slow push-in on the subject, hold at the end.", "Static shot with a gentle handheld sway.", "Pan left to right, following the product into frame.", "Slow pull back to reveal the full scene.", "Quick zoom in for emphasis, then hold steady."]}, {"key": "duration", "label": "Duration (sec)", "type": "pill", "options": ["4", "6", "8", "10", "15", "30"], "optionHelp": {"4": "What: a very short beat. When to use: for a quick cutaway or punchy insert. Example: a fast reaction shot between longer scenes.", "6": "What: a brief, single-idea shot. When to use: for a simple action with no dialogue. Example: a quick unboxing moment.", "8": "What: a short scene with time for one clear action or line. When to use: the standard length for a simple dialogue or action beat. Example: someone delivering one short line to camera.", "10": "What: enough time for a small action plus a beat of reaction. When to use: the default for most standard scenes. Example: opening a product and reacting to it.", "15": "What: enough time for a short sequence of actions or a fuller line of dialogue. When to use: for scenes with more than one beat. Example: picking up a product, examining it, then smiling.", "30": "What: a longer, fuller scene. When to use: for a hero/opening scene or one with multiple beats and dialogue. Example: a full testimonial statement with setup and payoff."}}, {"key": "broll", "label": "B-roll", "type": "pill", "options": ["None", "Product Macro Shot", "Product Spin", "Slow-Motion Detail", "Texture Close-up", "Lifestyle Cutaway"], "optionHelp": {"None": "What: no supplementary footage. When to use: the main shot alone tells the story. Example: a simple testimonial clip, no product insert needed.", "Product Macro Shot": "What: an extreme close-up insert of the product. When to use: to highlight fine detail like label or texture. Example: a close-up of a jar''s lid twisting open.", "Product Spin": "What: a rotating, 360°-style shot of the product. When to use: to show its full form and branding. Example: a bottle slowly rotating to reveal its label.", "Slow-Motion Detail": "What: a slowed-down insert emphasizing a small action. When to use: for a satisfying, premium-feel moment. Example: cream dripping slowly off a spoon.", "Texture Close-up": "What: a tight shot on surface texture. When to use: to emphasize quality or ingredients. Example: a close-up on the weave of a fabric.", "Lifestyle Cutaway": "What: a brief shot of the product in real use. When to use: to show it fitting into daily life. Example: someone casually sipping the drink at their desk."}}, {"key": "transition", "label": "Transition", "type": "pill", "options": ["Hard Cut", "Whip Pan", "Match Cut", "Fade", "Zoom Transition"], "optionHelp": {"Hard Cut": "What: an instant cut with no effect. When to use: the default choice for most edits, keeps pacing snappy. Example: cutting straight from the product shot to the customer''s reaction.", "Whip Pan": "What: a fast, blurred camera swipe between shots. When to use: for energetic, fast-paced transitions. Example: whipping from the kitchen to the dining table.", "Match Cut": "What: a cut where the next shot''s composition or action matches the previous one. When to use: for a smooth, clever visual continuity. Example: a jar lid closing cuts to a door closing in the next scene.", "Fade": "What: the shot gradually fades to/from black or the next shot. When to use: for a soft, emotional, or scene-ending transition. Example: fading out after the final call-to-action line.", "Zoom Transition": "What: a quick zoom into or out of frame to transition. When to use: for punchy, modern social-style edits. Example: zooming into the logo before cutting to the next scene."}}]')
)
WHERE stage = 5;
